# A gate reaches the people it waits on, in Slack.
#
# This is the whole of it: a rule that selects pending questions and derives one message per
# person each waits on. Nothing here knows what a gate IS — the record says who is waiting
# (waitingOn), what is being asked (pose) and what it is about (about), and the Slack iod draws
# those lines itself from the record every pass. What this file decides is only WHO is reached
# and WHERE, which is the part that belongs to a deployment rather than to a program.
#
# WHY A CHARTER AND NOT CODE: a question is a record, the engine already fans out over a list,
# and "one message per person" is a derive. Written in Go it would have been a second scheduler
# with its own idea of who is waiting; written here, an answer that moves `waitingOn` moves the
# messages on the next firing, and a reader can see the rule that sends them.
#
# INSTALLED AS THE SYSTEM (deploy's `systemCharter`, VIFF_SYSTEM_CHARTER). A rule acts as its
# installer, and a person's ask is read only by whom it asks and who may read what it is about
# (docs/asking.md): installed by anybody else, the rule would not see the asks put to people.

# The docket's own stratum is the system's, `asks` (internal/viffd/system.tony), which every viff instance has; the
# messages go strictly above it.
strata:
- name: gate-asked
  level: 100030
  match: {path: "slack.example.out"}
rules:
# A QUESTION A RULE OR A TASK RAISED, one message per person it waits on; a person's ask and an
# onward ask have rules of their own below, so each line names who asked the way that kind of
# asker is named. A question says who raised it in `from`: `{rule, at}`, `{task}` or
# `{principal}`, exactly one (docs/asking.md).
- name: gate-ask
  stratum: asks
  condition:
    # Pending, and NAMING WHOM IT WAITS ON. A question that named nobody waits on whoever the
    # docket admits — `waitingOn: [user:*]`, which is every person and so nobody to DM — and is
    # answered from the docket rather than in somebody's messages.
    #
    # `to: {grantees: null}` is a PRESENCE test and reads backwards at first glance: a null in a
    # predicate matches any value, so the only thing it constrains is that the field is there.
    # "Absent" is the other spelling, `!not {to: {grantees: null}}` (internal/trigger/match.go).
    #
    # And NOT ONE FOR A CHANNEL: an ask carrying a `slack` label is put to a channel, once, by
    # gate-ask-channel, and messages nobody in particular.
    subject:
      path: "mirror.ask.*"
      value: !and
      - {status: pending, to: {grantees: null}, from: {rule: null}}
      - !not {labels: {slack: null}}
  action:
    derive:
    # ONE PER PERSON, keyed by the principal, so the same person waiting on the same question
    # keeps one message however often the rule fires, and a question that changes hands writes
    # the new answerer's rather than editing somebody else's.
    - path: "slack.example.out"
      value: !each
        over: .[subject.value.waitingOn]
        key: .[item]
        value:
          to: .[item]
          asks: .[subject.value.id]
          # THE ONLY WORDS THIS RULE PUTS IN THE MESSAGE. Everything else under it — what is
          # asked, what it is about, who else is waiting, how to answer — the iod draws from the
          # question's own record each pass, so it stays true as the question moves. This line
          # is what a notification shows before anybody opens it, so it says why THEY were
          # reached and names the rule that reached them.
          text: "$[subject.value.from.rule] is waiting on you."
# A task's question, as gate-ask is a rule's: the line names the task.
- name: gate-ask-task
  stratum: asks
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "mirror.ask.*"
      value: !and
      - {status: pending, to: {grantees: null}, from: {task: null}}
      - !not {labels: {slack: null}}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "slack.example.out"
      value: !each
        over: .[subject.value.waitingOn]
        key: .[item]
        value:
          to: .[item]
          asks: .[subject.value.id]
          text: "$[subject.value.from.task] is waiting on you."
# An ask, which a principal raised — a person, an agent, or a place acting (`via:`): the line names
# who asked. A person's ask is DM'd whatever its labels. An onward ask is gate-ask-onward's.
- name: gate-ask-person
  stratum: asks
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "mirror.ask.*"
      value: !and
      - {status: pending, to: {grantees: null}, from: {principal: null}}
      - !not {serves: null}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "slack.example.out"
      value: !each
        over: .[subject.value.waitingOn]
        key: .[item]
        value:
          to: .[item]
          asks: .[subject.value.id]
          text: "$[subject.value.from.principal] is asking you."
# An onward ask, which a resolver raised to serve another ask: the line names the resolver and the
# party it asks for.
- name: gate-ask-onward
  stratum: asks
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "mirror.ask.*"
      value: {status: pending, to: {grantees: null}, from: {principal: null}, serves: null}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "slack.example.out"
      value: !each
        over: .[subject.value.waitingOn]
        key: .[item]
        value:
          to: .[item]
          asks: .[subject.value.id]
          text: "$[subject.value.from.principal] asks for $[subject.value.serves.for]."
# A GATE'S QUESTION FOR A CHANNEL: one message in the channel its `slack` label names, and a reply
# in that message's thread answers it as whoever typed it, held to whom it waits on. A gate puts
# its question to a channel by naming `answerers: ["user:*"]` and the label (merge.tony's
# merge-to-main): anyone whose Slack account is a viff user may answer, and nobody is DM'd.
#
# A RULE'S OR A TASK'S: the iod draws a channel's message reading the question as itself, and a
# gate's question is read as its subject is, where a person's ask is read only by its asker and
# whom it asks. A person's ask is DM'd, whatever its labels.
- name: gate-ask-channel
  stratum: asks
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "mirror.ask.*"
      value: {status: pending, from: {rule: null}, labels: {slack: null}}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "slack.example.out"
      id: .[subject.value.id]
      value:
        to: .[subject.value.labels.slack]
        asks: .[subject.value.id]
        text: "$[subject.value.from.rule] is asking here."
# A task's question for a channel, as gate-ask-channel is a rule's: the line names the task.
- name: gate-ask-task-channel
  stratum: asks
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "mirror.ask.*"
      value: {status: pending, from: {task: null}, labels: {slack: null}}
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "slack.example.out"
      id: .[subject.value.id]
      value:
        to: .[subject.value.labels.slack]
        asks: .[subject.value.id]
        text: "$[subject.value.from.task] is asking here."
